.\n\nHow many ways are there to put 4 balls in 3 boxes if the balls are distinguishable but the boxes are not?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"267a91be120f1dfbd946fc3b42bace5f4cb66183da3b0e98a0a257c4ef831dd0"},"seconds":33.38886380195618,"seed":393608842436103089,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to consider the different ways 4 distinguishable balls can be distributed into 3 indistinguishable boxes. We will look at the possible partitions of 4 into up to 3 parts, and then determine the number of ways to assign the balls to these partitions.\n\n### Step 1: Identify all possible partitions of 4 balls into up to 3 parts.\nThe possible partitions of 4 into up to 3 parts are:\n1. 4 (all in one box)\n2. 3 + 1 (three in one box, one in another)\n3. 2 + 2 (two in one box, two in another)\n4. 2 + 1 + 1 (two in one box, one in each of the other two)\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the number of ways to assign balls to each partition.\n1. **Partition 4:**\n   - All 4 balls in one box.\n   - There is only 1 way to do this.\n\n2. **Partition 3+1:**\n   - Choose 3 out of 4 balls to go into one box, and the remaining ball goes into another box.\n   - Number of ways to choose 3 balls out of 4: \\(\\binom{4}{3} = 4\\).\n\n3. **Partition 2+2:**\n   - Choose 2 out of 4 balls to go into one box, and the remaining 2 balls go into another box.\n   - Number of ways to choose 2 balls out of 4: \\(\\binom{4}{2} = 6\\).\n   - Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we divide by 2 to avoid double-counting: \\(\\frac{6}{2} = 3\\).\n\n4. **Partition 2+1+1:**\n   - Choose 2 out of 4 balls to go into one box, and the remaining two balls go into the other two boxes (one ball in each).\n   - Number of ways to choose 2 balls out of 4: \\(\\binom{4}{2} = 6\\).\n\n### Step 3: Sum the number of ways for each partition.\n- Partition 4: 1 way\n- Partition 3+1: 4 ways\n- Partition 2+2: 3 ways\n- Partition 2+1+1: 6 ways\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\\[ 1 + 4 + 3 + 6 = 14 \\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{14}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve this problem, we need to consider the different ways 4 distinguishable balls can be distributed into 3 indistinguishable boxes. We will look at the possible partitions of 4 into up to 3 parts, and then determine the number of ways to assign the balls to these partitions.\n\n### Step 1: Identify all possible partitions of 4 balls into up to 3 parts.\nThe possible partitions of 4 into up to 3 parts are:\n1. 4 (all in one box)\n2. 3 + 1 (three in one box, one in another)\n3. 2 + 2 (two in one box, two in another)\n4. 2 + 1 + 1 (two in one box, one in each of the other two)\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the number of ways to assign balls to each partition.\n1. **Partition 4:**\n   - All 4 balls in one box.\n   - There is only 1 way to do this.\n\n2. **Partition 3+1:**\n   - Choose 3 out of 4 balls to go into one box, and the remaining ball goes into another box.\n   - Number of ways to choose 3 balls out of 4: \\(\\binom{4}{3} = 4\\).\n\n3. **Partition 2+2:**\n   - Choose 2 out of 4 balls to go into one box, and the remaining 2 balls go into another box.\n   - Number of ways to choose 2 balls out of 4: \\(\\binom{4}{2} = 6\\).\n   - Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we divide by 2 to avoid double-counting: \\(\\frac{6}{2} = 3\\).\n\n4. **Partition 2+1+1:**\n   - Choose 2 out of 4 balls to go into one box, and the remaining two balls go into the other two boxes (one ball in each).\n   - Number of ways to choose 2 balls out of 4: \\(\\binom{4}{2} = 6\\).\n\n### Step 3: Sum the number of ways for each partition.\n- Partition 4: 1 way\n- Partition 3+1: 4 ways\n- Partition 2+2: 3 ways\n- Partition 2+1+1: 6 ways\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\\[ 1 + 4 + 3 + 6 = 14 \\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]","id":"counting_and_probability:412"}
